From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 15:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --mem-disp writes a small displacement as its own
 operand of a guest access

The lifter writes a memory operand as one address expression with the
displacement last: MEM32(esp + 0x10), MEMF(esi + eax * 4 - 0x8). A
runtime whose guest-to-host address map is not a plain addition (a
PowerPC host that keeps the Xbox's 0x80000000 window beside user space)
has to map each of those addresses whole, so [esi + 4] and [esi + 8] pay
for the map twice and the compiler cannot share it between them.

--mem-disp (mem_disp.py) rewrites each function's MEMxx(base + disp),
disp a constant in [-0x8000, 0x8000) and base a plain sum of terms, into
MEMxx_D(base, disp). The template header defines MEMxx_D(b, d) as
MEMxx((b) + (d)), the same access, so the generated code means what it
did; a runtime can define it to map base once and add disp on the host.
A larger displacement (a table indexed by a register, where the
displacement is the pointer) keeps the old form. Off by default.
---
